  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSome shower activity is moving across the forecast \narea this afternoon bringing just some light rain amounts across \nnorthern Ohio. The main show for this evening and tonight will be \ncourtesy of an MCV moving through Indiana this afternoon. Activity \nahead of this feature is waning across Ohio and Indiana and a dry \nslot with clearing has developed, stretching from Fort Wayne to just \nnorthwest of Indianapolis. This will allow for some instability to \nbuild and eventually this mesoscale feature to generate new \nconvection with heavy rainfall and some thunder activity. The \ntrajectory of this feature and any convection will be into the \nsouthwest portion of the forecast area. With precipitable water \nvalues over 2 inches, rain will be heavy and efficient. With recent \nrainfall across the area, there is more susceptibility to flooding. \nTherefore, there is flash flooding potential through tonight. Have \ngone ahead and issued a Flood Watch for the most concerning areas. \nIn addition, some of this area is currently highlighted by a \nModerate Risk for Excessive Rainfall by WPC. The severe risk for \ntonight continues to trend down with rain working over the region \nand limiting the overall instability. There remains a Marginal Risk \nfor severe weather from SPC for the low end potential for a damaging \nwind gust.\n\nFor Sunday, another shortwave will move through the northwest flow \naloft and support a cold front through the area. Ahead of this \nfront, more shower and storm activity will be possible. There could \nbe a brief break in convective activity during the morning hours as \nthe MCV and associated convection leaves the region. This could \nallow for some instability to build and provide an environment more \nsuitable for widespread shower and storm activity later in the day. \nThe biggest concern will be the continued heavy rain threat that \ncould have some flash flooding implications. The current Flood Watch \nmay need to have some portions extended into Sunday. However, there \nwill also be a slightly greater severe weather threat as more \ninstability in a slightly better sheared environment could provide \nmore fuel for storms and help provide more of a damaging wind threat \nor potentially a tornado or two. Most of the area is a Day 2 \nMarginal Risk for severe weather from SPC with a small south portion \nof the area in a Slight Risk.\n\nIsolated to scattered rain showers may linger Sunday night into \nMonday morning across Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA cold front is expected to move through the forecast area on \nWednesday, bringing showers and storms to the area. There will \nbe less moisture and instability across the region compared to \nrecent days and largely expect activity to remain generic and \nsub-severe. Behind the cold front, there will be a firm pattern \nchange to more of a late summer/early fall air mass with high \ntemperatures in the 70s, lows in the 50s, and low humidity.",
